1
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:10,260
Okay, next section is autoimmune disorders. These are characterized by immune mediated damage of

2
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:15,940
self-tissues. The idea here is that the immune system begins to react against the self-antigen

3
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:21,060
and as it reacts against the self-antigen, there is damage to tissues. If we wanted to be a little

4
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:25,980
more precise, we could say primary immune mediated damage because if you think about it,

5
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:30,980
the immune system does damage tissues, for example, with infection. So let's say the patient gets a

6
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:35,840
viral infection and the immune system begins to destroy the virally infected cells. In that

7
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:42,120
particular case, we are damaging self-tissue, but the target is the virus. It's not the self-tissue,

8
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:47,200
whereas in this particular case, the target is a self-antigen that then results in the damage.

9
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:52,240
So that's kind of a subtle distinction. There's about a 1 to 2 percent prevalence of these disorders

10
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,400
in the United States, and so all of us are going to see this in our clinical practice.

11
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:02,800
The underlying premise of autoimmunity is that there is a loss of self-tolerance. To understand

12
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:07,760
this, let's go back to normal. So we have B-cells and we have T-cells, and of course the B-cells,

13
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:13,560
they recognize antigen via immunoglobulin, and the T-cells, they recognize antigen via the T-cell

14
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:21,380
receptor. Now, we produce a broad spectrum of T-cell receptors and immunoglobulin because we have to deal

15
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:27,420
with a broad spectrum of antigens that could come in from the outside world. And so we generate this

16
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:33,820
broad spectrum, but remember that the generation of immunoglobulin and T-cell receptor is random.

17
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:40,260
And so while we generate T-cell receptor and immunoglobulin, we also generate some T-cell receptor

18
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:45,180
and immunoglobulin that is reactive against self-antigens. And so that's why I stay here,

19
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:50,940
that self-reactive lymphocytes are regularly generated. Now, in order to deal with these self-reactive

20
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:56,380
lymphocytes, we have a process called tolerance, which helps to sort of take care of them, and we'll

21
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:02,460
talk about this in more detail moving forward. Tolerance can occur centrally, which means that it

22
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:08,120
occurs in the generative organs. It occurs in the organs in which the lymphocytes develop, or it can

23
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:13,860
occur peripherally, which means it occurs outside of the areas where the lymphocytes develop, instead in

24
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:19,760
the peripheral tissues. So let's start with central tolerance. Central tolerance will occur in two

25
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:25,060
places. It'll occur where the T-cells develop, which is the thymus, and it'll also occur where the B-cells

26
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:28,760
develop, which is going to be the bone marrow. So we're going to focus on the thymus, and then we'll

27
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:33,820
move to the bone marrow in a moment. Let's go back and review what happens in the thymus as the T-cells

28
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:39,320
develop. So the stem cells that are eventually to become T-cells come from the bone marrow, through

29
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:44,920
the blood, and eventually enter into the thymus. When they enter into the thymus, they undergo varying

30
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:49,960
stages of development, so that they eventually become double positive cells, and double positive

31
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:55,300
basically means that they express CD4 and CD8, that's why they're double positive, and they

32
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:01,220
also express T-cell receptor. Once they express T-cell receptor, they then eventually will progress

33
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:07,600
to become single positive, which means that they'll either express CD4 or CD8, and eventually,

34
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:19,600
exit as naive mature T-cells. So these naive mature T-cells would go out into the periphery.

35
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:28,600
Now, as the cells are developing, they undergo various checkpoints that allow them to move to

36
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:34,600
the next step. So for example, the double positive cells undergo something here, which is called

37
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:42,600
positive selection. Positive selection means that the double positive cells are checked to

38
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:49,600
see whether they can bind MHC and antigen, meaning do they bind antigen when it's bound to MHC?

39
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:55,600
So they have to be able to recognize MHC. If the double positive cells are able to recognize MHC,

40
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:01,600
they can progress to become single positive cells. If they can't, they undergo apoptosis and they die.

41
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:07,600
So that's called positive selection. It selects cells that are MHC-restricted, and that's how we

42
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:14,600
get MHC restriction within our T-cells. The single positive cells eventually undergo a checkpoint,

43
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:21,600
and that is called negative selection. And in negative selection, what happens is the question is asked,

44
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:29,600
do the single positive cells bind self-antigen? If they avidly bind self-antigen, then they undergo apoptosis,

45
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:36,600
and if they don't, then they're allowed to progress and exit from the thymus. Now, if you think about this,

46
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:41,600
negative selection is very important because by asking the question, do you bind self-antigen,

47
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:45,600
and then killing off those cells, we're actually removing self-reactive lymphocytes.

48
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:50,600
Remember, the T-cell receptor was generated randomly. It could be specific for self-antigen.

49
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:56,600
If it binds self-antigen within the thymus, then those cells will die via negative selection,

50
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:02,600
and we won't have to send them into the periphery so that they could theoretically attack self-tissues.

51
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:07,600
So negative selection is essential for the removal of self-reactive lymphocytes.

52
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:14,600
This is a little bit of a background concerning central tolerance, and I've mentioned that here in your text,

53
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:19,600
where I see that central tolerance occurs in the thymus, and of course that's going to be for the T-cells.

54
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:23,600
Now, just to highlight the importance of central tolerance, I've mentioned a very rare disorder,

55
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:30,600
which is called autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome, that can arise when central tolerance goes awry.

56
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:34,600
Remember the developmental sort of drawing that I had given you?

57
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:40,600
There's a stem cell which enters into the thymus, and then eventually becomes a double positive thymocyte

58
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:47,600
with CD4, CD8, and T-cell receptor, which then eventually becomes a single positive thymocyte,

59
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:55,600
which basically means that it's CD4 positive or CD8 positive, and eventually that will exit as a naive mature T-cell.

60
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:58,600
So this is the normal that we've already mentioned.

61
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:04,600
Now at this point, as the single positive cell is heading towards the exit of the thymus,

62
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:07,600
it will undergo a process called negative selection.

63
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:17,600
And negative selection, it's important to know, that negative selection actually occurs here in the medulla of the thymus,

64
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,600
this being the cortex.

65
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:26,600
Now, in order for negative selection to occur, these thymocytes must be exposed to antigen

66
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:30,600
that would normally be present in the periphery, in the body.

67
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:41,600
So for example, there are dendritic cells present within this medulla that express a wide array of self-antigen on MHC,

68
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:44,600
so that the cells can be tested to see whether they bind self-antigen.

69
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:57,600
And there is also a cell called the medullary epithelial cell that also expresses a wide array of peripheral antigens on MHC.

70
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:04,600
These can also be shown to the lymphocyte in order to see whether it binds those self-antigens.

71
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:10,600
Now, of course, if the lymphocyte binds the self-antigens either on the dendritic cell or on the medullary epithelial cell,

72
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,600
then the, and by the way, this is a medullary epithelial cell,

73
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:20,600
then if it binds these self-antigens, then the cell will undergo apoptosis and will die.

74
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,600
And that's how we'll get rid of self-reactive lymphocytes.

75
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:28,600
Now, the medullary epithelial cell, in order to express some of these self-antigens,

76
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:32,600
has a transcription factor in the nucleus, which is called air.

77
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:36,600
Air is necessary for expression of some of these self-antigens.

78
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:43,600
Now, imagine, if we don't have air, we won't be able to express some subset of self-antigens.

79
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:50,600
If we can't express some subset of self-antigens, then lymphocytes that are generated against that subset

80
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:56,600
will be able to escape out into the periphery and will have self-reactive lymphocytes within the periphery.

81
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:00,600
And that's what happens in this disorder called autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome.

82
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:08,600
Patients have air mutations, and so, therefore, lymphocytes that are reactive to antigens that are dependent upon air

83
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:17,600
will be able to escape, and in particular, these patients develop autoimmunity that destroys endocrine glands.

84
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,600
In fact, there's a triad that's seen in this disorder.

85
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:24,600
The first is that the patients get hypoparathyroidism.

86
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:29,600
The second is that the patients get adrenal failure.

87
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:36,600
And the third problem that these patients get is that they get chronic candida infections of the skin and the oral mucosa.

88
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:42,600
So, this is the triad, and so you can see here that it's due to the autoimmunity that you get destruction of the parathyroids

89
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:47,600
and destruction of the adrenals that then results in this disorder.

90
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:53,600
Now, moving on to the bone marrow, central tolerance also occurs in the bone marrow, and, of course, that's going to be for the B cells.

91
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:56,600
Now, when you think about the B cells, it's kind of very similar.

92
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:00,600
Here we are in the bone marrow. The stem cell is actually generated in the bone marrow.

93
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:05,600
So, it simply is in the bone marrow, and then it progresses, and it becomes an immature B cell.

94
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:14,600
And then, it progresses from there to eventually exit the bone marrow as a naive, mature B cell.

95
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:21,600
So, that's sort of the very simplistic normal development that occurs within the bone marrow.

96
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:28,600
Now, as the cell progresses from a stem cell to an immature B cell, it's going to express immunoglobulin.

97
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:37,600
And then, the question is going to be asked, somewhere out here, via negative selection, do you bind self-antigen too tightly?

98
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:42,600
So, there are dendritic cells that are present within the bone marrow that express self-antigen.

99
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:52,600
And, as they express self-antigen, the immunoglobulin will be exposed to it, and if it binds too tightly, then the cells will undergo one of two things.

100
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:59,600
Now, the first thing that can happen as a result of negative selection is that the B cells can undergo receptor editing.

101
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:11,600
So, what happens in receptor editing is that the RAG genes that are essential for the development of the immunoglobulin specificities will be re-expressed.

102
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:18,600
So, as you get re-expression of the RAG genes, which will then allow for editing of the light chain of the immunoglobulin.

103
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:27,600
And, once the light chain gets edited, the hope is that it will no longer bind self-antigen, and so that would allow the B cell to remain functional and go out into the periphery.

104
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:34,600
So, one possibility that can occur through negative selection is receptor editing via re-expression of RAG genes.

105
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:39,600
Another possibility is that if you can't edit the receptor, is that you simply undergo apoptosis.

106
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:45,600
So, cells here can also undergo apoptosis, and that would be through the mitochondrial pathway.

107
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:53,600
So, anyway, this again is highlighting the importance of tolerance and the way by which we protect ourselves against self-reactive lymphocytes.

108
00:10:53,600 --> 00:11:01,600
Now, let's assume for a moment that the central tolerance mechanisms fail, or that some rare cells are able to escape central tolerance.

109
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:05,600
Well, we have a second line of defense, and that is called peripheral tolerance.

110
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:15,600
And, what happens in peripheral tolerance is that if an antigen is recognized in the peripheral tissues without a co-stimulation, without a co-stimulatory signal,

111
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:22,600
then the lymphocyte will undergo energy, which means it will shut down, or it will undergo apoptosis.

112
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:28,600
So, just to highlight this, let's just imagine for a moment that you have a CD4-positive T cell,

113
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:37,600
and the CD4-positive T cell is using its T cell receptor in order to bind antigen being presented by this dendritic cell.

114
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:45,600
So, here is your MHC2 with the associated antigen, and you've got this sort of first interaction occurring.

115
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:53,600
Now, when this first interaction occurs, it also requires a second interaction in order to activate the T cell.

116
00:11:53,600 --> 00:12:00,600
So, for example, in this particular instance, the second interaction would be that CD28, on the T cell,

117
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:04,600
would bind a molecule called B7 being expressed by the dendritic cell.

118
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:12,600
This would provide the second signal, and it's when the second signal arises that the CD4-positive T cell would actually become activated.

119
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:17,600
Now, let's imagine that there's a self-reactive CD4-positive T cell that's floating around.

120
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:24,600
It goes into the periphery, and it binds, using its T cell receptor, it binds some peripheral antigen.

121
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:28,600
Now, when it does that, you would expect that there would be no second signal,

122
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:31,600
because there's no inflammation or anything generating a second signal.

123
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:37,600
And so, if there's no second signal, then this CD4-positive T cell will undergo energy.

124
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:41,600
And energy, of course, just means that the cell basically shuts down and goes to sleep.

125
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:43,600
So, that's one possibility.

126
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:50,600
Now, another possibility is that this CD4-positive T cell regularly and consistently keeps binding antigen.

127
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:56,600
It repeatedly binds antigen without this secondary, without this co-stimulatory second signal.

128
00:12:56,600 --> 00:13:03,600
And in that particular case, then the other possibility would be that this CD4-positive T cell will undergo apoptosis.

129
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:08,600
And the apoptosis occurs in a very interesting way.

130
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:14,600
The CD4-positive T cell has a receptor present on the cell, which is called CD95.

131
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:18,600
It's also known as FAS, and this is a death receptor.

132
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:26,600
If you have regular binding of the T cell receptor to antigen without the regular and consistent binding,

133
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:32,600
without the second signal, then the CD4-positive T cell will eventually begin to express FAS ligand.

134
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:38,600
And when it expresses FAS ligand, FAS ligand will bind the FAS receptor, which is CD95,

135
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:45,600
and that is a death receptor, which will then indicate to the cell that it needs to undergo apoptosis.

136
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:50,600
At the same time, this FAS ligand, let's say that there's another B cell or T cell nearby.

137
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:53,600
This would be another T cell or another B cell.

138
00:13:53,600 --> 00:14:00,600
If this FAS ligand were to bind CD95 on another T cell or CD95 on another B cell,

139
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:05,600
it would also induce apoptosis of other B and T cells that might be self-reactive as well.

140
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:10,600
So, essentially, there's two ways by which peripheral tolerance occurs.

141
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:16,600
Number one, through energy, and number two, through apoptosis.

142
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:22,600
In order to highlight the importance of peripheral tolerance, I want to mention a very rare syndrome again.

143
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:26,600
This is called autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome.

144
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:34,600
And, basically, what happens in autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome is that you have mutations in the FAS apoptosis pathway.

145
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:39,600
So, remember, let's go back. Let's say that this is that CD4 positive T cell.

146
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:46,600
So, of course, you need to have the receptor, which was CD95, also called FAS.

147
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:51,600
You need to be able to have the ligand, which was called FAS ligand.

148
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:57,600
And, you also need to be able to have the caspases that are necessary or that get activated through this pathway.

149
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:01,600
In particular, one caspase that's important is caspase 10.

150
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:11,600
So, imagine if a patient has a mutation such as they lose FAS, or, more rarely, if they have a mutation where they lose FAS ligand or CD10,

151
00:15:11,600 --> 00:15:17,600
they would no longer be able to undergo this apoptotic pathway in the periphery.

152
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:22,600
And, therefore, that would allow for the survival of some self-reactive lymphocytes.

153
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:28,600
And so, in this particular disorder that we're discussing, there is mutations in the FAS apoptosis pathway.

154
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:34,600
Again, the more common mutation being in FAS itself, but it can also occur in FAS ligand or caspase 10.

155
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:40,600
And, that results in something called autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome.

156
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:46,600
Now, the autoimmune basically implies that the patients have cells that are self-reactive lymphocytes.

157
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:54,600
And, actually, what happens is that these cells generate immunoglobulin, in particular, IgG, against cells in the blood.

158
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:59,600
And so, these patients can get cytopenias. For example, they can get an anemia or a thrombocytopenia.

159
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:05,600
Where the lymphoproliferative part of the syndrome implies that they get proliferation of the lymphocytes,

160
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:08,600
because they can't get rid of these self-reactive lymphocytes.

161
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:13,600
And so, those self-reactive lymphocytes can actually proliferate and expand the lymphoid compartments.

162
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:18,600
And so, for example, areas where lymphocytes normally live will become enlarged.

163
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:23,600
For example, they get something called lymphadenopathy, or enlargement of the lymph nodes throughout the body.

164
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:26,600
It's a generalized lymphadenopathy.

165
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:28,600
They also get hepatosplenomegaly.

166
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:31,600
And, because there's so much proliferation of the lymphoid cells,

167
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:35,600
eventually mutations can be picked up and these patients can eventually get lymphoma.

168
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:40,600
Now, again, you're never going to see this disorder because it's extremely rare.

169
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:47,600
But, why it's important is because it highlights the essence of peripheral tolerance

170
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:51,600
and how important it is in getting rid of self-reactive lymphocytes.

171
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:56,600
And so, this is something to be aware of as well, especially because it ties into some basic immunology.

172
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:02,600
Now, we do have a third way by which we can protect ourselves against self-reactive lymphocytes.

173
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:07,600
And that is through a special subset of T cells called regulatory T cells.

174
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:13,600
Regulatory T cells are CD4 positive T cells that suppress immune responses.

175
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:15,600
They actually act in two ways.

176
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:19,600
The first way in which they act is that they can block T cell activation.

177
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:24,600
So, for example, let's say that this is the CD4 positive T cell.

178
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:28,600
And this is the dendritic cell that is presenting the antigen.

179
00:17:28,600 --> 00:17:38,600
So, you've got your T cell receptor and you've got this MHC class II molecule that has antigen that the T cells responding against.

180
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:45,600
Now, let's imagine for a second that this is a T cell that is against a self-antigen.

181
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:47,600
So, it's a self-reactive lymphocyte.

182
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:54,600
Of course, it's going to need the second signal, CD28, which would bind B7.

183
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:58,600
But what can happen is that you can have a regulatory T cell come in.

184
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:00,600
So, this is a Treg.

185
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:06,600
And the regulatory T cells actually express a molecule called CTLA4.

186
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:08,600
CTLA4.

187
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:12,600
And CTLA4 can compete and can actually bind B7.

188
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:19,600
So, as CTLA4 competes and binds B7, that decreases the probability of a second signal.

189
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:23,600
And therefore, these T cells might, for example, undergo energy.

190
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:30,600
Another way by which the regulatory T cells can inhibit immune responses is by secreting cytokines.

191
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:38,600
So, two cytokines that they secrete are, number one, IL-10, and number two, TGF-beta.

192
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:42,600
IL-10 is important because it inhibits macrophages and dendritic cells.

193
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:47,600
In fact, in particular, it inhibits or limits the expression of MHC class II.

194
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:52,600
So, if you limit the expression of MHC class II, obviously, you don't have much for the T cell receptor to bind.

195
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:57,600
And similarly, IL-10 also limits the production of costumulatory molecules.

196
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:00,600
So, you would limit the production of B7, for example.

197
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:05,600
And that would also, again, decrease the interaction or the ability to activate these self-reactive T cells.

198
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:12,600
And then, of course, the second cytokine is TGF-beta, and TGF-beta also inhibits activation of macrophages.

199
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:17,600
So, this is a little bit of a summary of the way in which regulatory T cells work.

200
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:23,600
Now, when you think about regulatory T cells, there's kind of a phenotype that you should be aware of.

201
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:35,600
They are CD4 positive, CD25 positive, and FOXP3 positive.

202
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:39,600
CD4 indicates that they are CD4 positive T cells.

203
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:46,600
CD25 is also known as IL-2 receptor.

204
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:55,600
And this helps me to remember that the CD, that the regulatory T cells are dependent on IL-2 for growth and survival.

205
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:58,600
So, this is important.

206
00:19:58,600 --> 00:20:01,600
Again, CD25 being part of the IL-2 receptor.

207
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:12,600
And then, finally, the FOXP3, FOXP3 is a transcription factor, and regulatory T cells are dependent on FOXP3 for both their development and their maintenance.

208
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:18,600
So, this is a little bit of the phenotype that you should be aware of concerning regulatory T cells.

209
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:25,600
Now, let's go back to the slide for a minute, because I want to show you the importance of regulatory T cells as far as human disease is concerned.

210
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:32,600
One of the things that we know is that CD25 polymorphisms are associated with autoimmunity.

211
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:37,600
For example, there's a relationship between multiple sclerosis and type 1 diabetes.

212
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:43,600
Now, one explanation of this could be that CD25 is essential for regulatory T cells.

213
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:50,600
Remember that there's CD4 positive, CD25 positive, and FOXP3 positive.

214
00:20:50,600 --> 00:21:01,600
So, if we have polymorphisms of CD25, which is part of the IL-2 receptor, and IL-2 is essential for development and survival of regulatory T cells,

215
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:10,600
perhaps CD25 polymorphisms are affecting the regulatory T cell subset and, therefore, resulting in autoimmunity.

216
00:21:10,600 --> 00:21:19,600
So, there is an association of CD25 polymorphisms with autoimmunity, for example, multiple sclerosis and type 1 diabetes.

217
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:29,600
Another important thing to recognize is that FOXP3 mutations result in autoimmunity, in particular, a very particular syndrome called IPEX syndrome.

218
00:21:29,600 --> 00:21:41,600
The I stands for immune dysfunction, the P stands for polyendocrinopathy, the E stands for enteropathy, and the X stands for X-linked.

219
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:47,600
And so, what happens in these patients is that they get autoimmune dysfunction, which then results in endocrinopathy.

220
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:57,600
And the endocrinopathy can be thyroiditis, with inflammation and destruction of the thyroid gland, or type 1 diabetes, with inflammation and destruction of the islets and the pancreas.

221
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:03,600
And they also get autoimmune enteropathy, which then leads to diarrhea.

222
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:10,600
So, this is another example of the immune system damaging tissues, whether they be endocrine or whether they be in the gut.

223
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:14,600
And then finally, the X represents the fact that this is an X-linked syndrome.

224
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:18,600
These patients usually also have eczema of the skin, and it usually arises in an infant.

225
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:24,600
So, again, very rare syndromes, but highlight the importance of regulatory T cells in this discussion.

226
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:27,600
A few more very quick principles.

227
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:30,600
Autoimmune disorders tend to be more common in women.

228
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:33,600
They classically will affect women of childbearing age.

229
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:38,600
One theory here is that estrogen reduces the apoptosis of self-reactive B cells.

230
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:45,600
Remember that self-reactive B cells will undergo apoptosis, either in the bone marrow or in the periphery, when they bind self-antigen.

231
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:50,600
So, if you have estrogen and it blocks that apoptosis, that would increase the risk of autoimmunity.

232
00:22:50,600 --> 00:22:54,600
And, of course, there's a lot of estrogen in women of childbearing age.

233
00:22:54,600 --> 00:23:06,600
Another important principle concerning autoimmunity is that it's our current sort of understanding that this arises in a genetically susceptible individual who is exposed to an environmental trigger.

234
00:23:06,600 --> 00:23:13,600
Now, as far as the genetic susceptibility is concerned, the reason why we believe that is because we know that there's an increased incidence in twins.

235
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:17,600
So, that implies that there's some sort of genetic component to these disorders.

236
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:23,600
When you study the genes that may be responsible, the strongest association is with HLA subtypes.

237
00:23:23,600 --> 00:23:33,600
Now, HLA are the genes that code for MHC, and so it kind of makes sense because most autoimmune disorders are against protein,

238
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:38,600
and so you would think, or it would make sense, that the HLA subtypes would be important.

239
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:49,600
In particular, among the HLA subtypes, the strongest association among HLA subtypes is with HLAB27,

240
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:53,600
and its association with a disorder called ankylosing spondylitis.

241
00:23:53,600 --> 00:24:02,600
Now, we're going to cover that later in the course, but I just want to highlight the strongest association with HLA subtypes is with that particular subtype, HLAB27,

242
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:05,600
with a disorder called ankylosing spondylitis.

243
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:14,600
When you look at non-HLA genes, then one of the strongest associations, or we can say perhaps one of the genes that's most frequently linked to autoimmunity,

244
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:23,600
is a gene called PTPN22, and so polymorphisms of PTPN22 are also associated with autoimmunity.

245
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:35,600
Now, PTPN22 is actually a tyrosine phosphatase, and when you, the mutations actually result in a, or the polymorphisms I should say, result in a gain of function,

246
00:24:35,600 --> 00:24:39,600
which decreases the signaling necessary for tolerance.

247
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:44,600
And so, that's sort of one of the theories, is that in order to develop tolerance, you need to have signaling.

248
00:24:44,600 --> 00:24:50,600
For example, the cell needs to bind self-antigen, and then a signal is sent telling the cell to undergo energy.

249
00:24:50,600 --> 00:25:00,600
However, if the signaling mechanisms are awry, for example, with a PTPN22 polymorphism, then that would increase the risk of developing autoimmunity.

250
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:05,600
So, these are some genes that, that could be potentially associated with autoimmunity.

251
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:10,600
Now, at the same time, it's not enough to have genetic susceptibility, there also needs to be an environmental trigger.

252
00:25:10,600 --> 00:25:13,600
To get a better handle on this, let's go back to the whiteboard.

253
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:24,600
So, you've got this patient who has a genetic susceptibility, and we assume that the genetic susceptibility is going to result in some sort of loss of self-tolerance.

254
00:25:24,600 --> 00:25:31,600
And when there's a loss of tolerance, we assume that the patient is going to generate some sort of self-reactive lymphocyte.

255
00:25:31,600 --> 00:25:37,600
Now, this self-reactive lymphocyte, one or two, for example, is not going to be able to generate autoimmunity.

256
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:45,600
In order to get autoimmunity, we're going to need expansion of the population of self-reactive lymphocytes, along with activation of the self-reactive lymphocytes.

257
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:54,600
And so, in order for that to occur, we also require, along with genetic susceptibility, we also require an environmental trigger.

258
00:25:54,600 --> 00:26:03,600
Some examples of triggers, one would be that the patient simply gets an infection with, for example, some other virus or bacteria.

259
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:09,600
And when they get this infection, that revs up the immune system and activates the antigen-presenting cells.

260
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:20,600
And as a sort of side effect of the inflammation, or almost as a bystander, this self-reactive lymphocyte gets activated, and that then can result in autoimmunity.

261
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:26,600
So, that's called bystander activation, because the self-reactive lymphocyte gets activated sort of as a bystander.

262
00:26:26,600 --> 00:26:41,600
Another possibility is this self-reactive lymphocyte gets exposed, or let's say that the patient gets exposed, to a bacteria that has a molecule that also happens to activate this self-reactive lymphocyte.

263
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:45,600
So, the bacteria has a molecule that's similar to a self-antigen.

264
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:56,600
So, when this bacteria comes in, it infects the patient, the self-reactive lymphocyte will get activated, and then will eventually be able to produce autoimmunity as well.

265
00:26:56,600 --> 00:27:10,600
And this is called molecular mimicry, because the antigen on the microbe mimics or resembles a self-antigen, which then allows for expansion of the, or activation of the self-reactive lymphocyte.

266
00:27:10,600 --> 00:27:17,600
So, those are two examples of triggers, either bystander activation or molecular mimicry.

267
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:25,600
The final point that I would like to make here is that it's very important to recognize that triggers are essential.

268
00:27:25,600 --> 00:27:37,600
For example, when patients have genetic susceptibility, for example, let's say that the patients have HLA-B27, that does not mean that the patient is automatically going to develop autoimmunity.

269
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:42,600
In fact, most patients with HLA-B27 actually never develop autoimmune disorders.

270
00:27:42,600 --> 00:27:47,600
So, again, that just highlights the importance of the role of this trigger.

271
00:27:47,600 --> 00:27:54,600
The final point that I would like to make is that these disorders, i.e. autoimmune disorders, are generally clinically progressive.

272
00:27:54,600 --> 00:27:58,600
Now, what happens is the patients tend to get relapses and remissions.

273
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:03,600
And the reason they get relapses and remissions is because the antigen is always present.

274
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:06,600
And so, you get exposure to the antigen.

275
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:08,600
You get amplification of the immune system.

276
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:10,600
That results in a flare.

277
00:28:10,600 --> 00:28:13,600
Eventually, the immune response remits.

278
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:14,600
The patient goes along.

279
00:28:14,600 --> 00:28:19,600
And then, all of a sudden, there's a relapse with a flare with amplification of the immune response.

280
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:25,600
And so, that's a classic presentation of autoimmune disorders that they relapse and they remit.

281
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:31,600
And then, finally, the last point that I would like to make is that often there's overlapping features between autoimmune disorders.

282
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:34,600
Now, we're going to study several autoimmune disorders in a few minutes.

283
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:37,600
And you're going to see that sometimes the features can overlap.

284
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:42,600
And the reason that features can overlap is due to something called epitope spreading.

285
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:47,600
So, imagine that the patient has a self-reactive lymphocyte that is reacting against the self-antigen.

286
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:52,600
When those self-reactive lymphocytes get activated and expand, they'll destroy the tissue.

287
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:57,600
And when they destroy the tissue, new antigens that the immune system hasn't normally seen are going to be exposed.

288
00:28:57,600 --> 00:29:01,600
And so, other lymphocytes can begin to react against those new antigens.

289
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:04,600
And that can result in overlapping features in these disorders.

290
00:29:04,600 --> 00:29:13,600
So, the exposure of new antigens with the subsequent spreading of the immune response to additional antigens is called epitope spreading.

291
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:20,600
And epitope spreading can be one explanation of why we might see overlapping features in the disorders that we're going to cover.

292
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:24,600
So, that concludes this theoretical portion of autoimmunity.

293
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:28,600
The last point that I'd like to make actually concerns here what's on the bottom of the slide.

294
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:31,600
I think it's pretty obvious if anybody were to read it.

295
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:40,600
However, we do appreciate your respecting the legal copyright along with the ethical copyright that goes along with the Pathoma materials.

296
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:47,600
So, again, thank you for making Pathoma one of the most widely used pathology resources throughout the world.

